- #2 Make yourself unbeatable first: don't rely on the enemy not coming; rely on having what cannot be attacked. This applies: don't rely on the agent remembering time; make the guard reliable. Also "win first then seek battle" — arrange the outcome before the contest.
- #4 Know whether you can fight; know the other side and yourself. Knowing the ground permits battle — for the agent, knowledge of time. Five ways to know victory include being prepared and waiting for the unprepared.
- #5 Move only when it accords with advantage; never from anger. Non-利不動 — do not add a status line on every call unless it yields advantage. Also #6 mix harm into benefit.
- #6 mix harm into benefit — deliberation must contain both columns. So weigh the per-call cost (harm) against the pacing benefit (benefit).
- #7 Deception — not relevant here (no enemy). Could be "make the system show no form"? Not really.
- #8 Value victory not duration; speed. Relevant: the run is time-boxed; value finishing within the time, not prolonging. A status every call costs; also "clumsy speed vs skill prolonged". But careful: #8 says value victory over duration, and one measure of enemy grain = twenty of own (supply from the enemy). The research F2 shows showing budget every step reduced search calls 40.4% and cost 31.3% — that is "feeding on the enemy" in a sense: the reminder pays for itself in saved calls. That's a strong argument for B. Hmm, but the guard's line itself is added context; F2 says with the tracker, fewer search calls and lower cost. Actually F2's saving is in search calls, not tokens. F1's tracker adds a budget block after every tool response.
- #10 Avoid the solid, strike the empty; have no constant form. Could apply: strike where empty — don't add context on every call if context is precious? Hmm.
- #11 Foreknowledge must come from people; pay for it. This is very relevant: the agent's foreknowledge of time cannot come from the agent's own sense; it must come from people (workers) who know the situation — the workers' timestamped progress reports. And "pay for it" — do not begrudge the cost (ranks, salaries, gold) of knowing. That argues against being stingy with tokens; the ~20 tokens is the price of foreknowledge, and #11 says not paying it is the height of inhumanity (failure). This strongly supports B. Also F6: "LLMs struggle to internally track elapsed time" — so foreknowledge must come from outside.
- #14 Choose the action by the ratio of forces. Hmm, applies maybe to matching reminder frequency to remaining budget: with ten to one surround, etc. Actually the "ratio of forces" decides the action. Not directly.
- #17 Learn the enemy by probing and by signs. Applies: the guard reads the clock; per-call status is a "sign". Hmm.
- #15 general's faults: five dangers. Relevant: "one set on living can be captured" (fear of spending tokens?), "one who loves the people can be harried" (愛民可煩). Actually 愛民 — the general who loves the people can be troubled. Here, an agent that is too attached to being quiet/cheap can be harried. Hmm. Also the six defeats: 亂 (disorder) from weak general, unclear instruction. Could apply to C's unmeasured N.
- #18 Spirit: avoid keen spirit, strike when it flags — managing spirit. Applied to the agent's context budget? The design should avoid spending context when it's fresh? Hmm. Better: "managing strength: await the far-come while near, the weary while rested, the hungry while fed" — get the information when needed (hungry → fed). That supports giving time when the agent needs it, i.e., ongoing.
- #19 Name the ground, act as prescribed. Applies to condition mapping: if the ground is "time-boxed run", the prescribed action... The nine grounds. Could map: the run is like "heavy ground" (deep in hostile land) → plunder (take what you need from the environment)? Hmm, or "dead ground: fight" — when time is nearly up, fight. Actually the mapping: marks correspond to grounds. But #19 is about a table from conditions to responses. We could use: identify which ground you are in (how much time left) then act as prescribed — but the chapter's ground definitions are about terrain, so it's a stretch. Maybe use #19 for "the variations and the pattern of human feelings must be examined" — i.e., the policy should vary with the state, not be constant.
- #3 Whole over broken; strike plans first, walled cities last. Ranking: plans highest, walled cities lowest, attacking cities the last resort. This maps beautifully to options: the highest form is to arrange the whole (the agent finishes without needing a rescue); the lowest is a forced intervention at the end (deadline up, every call after). B is "伐謀" — shaping the agent's plan continuously so no battle (overrun) is needed. A treats the deadline as a walled city to attack at the end. Actually I can argue option B is the highest form: it wins by shaping continuous conduct, not by a final assault.
- #16 Orders clear first, then enforced; care that cannot command is useless. Applies: the reminder policy must be clear and consistently enforced (every call) — clear orders. Option C's unmeasured N is unclear orders; unclear orders are the general's fault. This argues against C. Very good fit: "If orders are not clear... it is the general's fault." C has "a number nobody has measured" — not clear.
- #13 Not every road taken, not every command accepted: could apply to deciding against B if it hurts. Hmm.
- #12 Seek it in 勢 not from the people: important! "The skilled fighter seeks victory in 勢 and does not demand it of his people" — for design: fix the system/guard (the 勢), don't demand the agent track time by willpower. And don't demand workers do extra? F6 says LLMs struggle to internally track time. So put it in the harness, not the agent. This supports the guard doing the work (which all options do). But among the options, B is the mechanism that doesn't demand the agent count.
- #9 Bring the other side to you: make time come to the agent rather than the agent seeking time. Supports B (guard pushes status) over the agent asking.
