# 孫子 (Sun Tzu) — how often the guard says how much time is left

## Answer
Take (B): the guard puts a one-line used/left figure into context after every tool call, and the fuller block with the workers' progress at 50%, 80% and 100%, then every call once the time is up. Keep the line numeric and short (the ~20 tokens), and let the run's measured hook cost (C6) decide only the fallback: if the per-call line is measured to cost more than it returns, fall back to (A) as the last resort — not to (C), whose N is unmeasured.

Hmm, "answer in one or two sentences; conditional if it must be".

## How I decided
1. Reckon first (my #1). The choice must win the two stated use cases — the agent knows how much of the limit is used, and sees the workers' progress per small piece — under C2/C3 and C6. I put the options against those: B serves both directly and its gain is measured (F2: 40.4% fewer search calls, 31.3% lower cost with the per-step tracker); A serves the first only at the marks and is untested (F1–F4, F6 all update per step; the research says marks-only is "not addressed"); C serves both only at an N nobody has measured.
2. Seek it in the ground, not in the agent (my #12) — F6: "LLMs struggle to internally track elapsed time." A design that asks the agent to feel time is asking the people; the guard must carry the 勢. All three use the guard, but B changes the agent's condition on every call.
3. Foreknowledge must come from people, and must be paid for (my #11). The workers' timestamped progress lines are the people who know the situation; the 20-token line is the rank and salary I must not begrudge. F6 measured the price of not paying: 4% vs 32% closure.
4. Do not rest on the agent noticing in time; rest on what you own (my #2). F5 and F8 show a budget stated once fails; the guard, forced after every call, is the part I own. 
5. Rank by 謀攻 (my #3): shaping conduct continuously is 伐謀; waiting for the marks and then the end is 攻城, the method for when there is no alternative. B is the former.
6. Do not move without advantage, do not act without gain (my #5). A's gain is untested and shows itself too late; C's gain is a number nobody has measured (非得不用); B's gain is the one measured. F1/F3/F4 vendor/harness designs chose per-step too.
7. Mix harm into benefit (my #6): benefit column — F2 (40.4% fewer calls, 31.3% lower cost), F6 (32% vs 4%); harm column — 20 tokens on every call, and F4 warns that a too-tight budget brings refusal or early stop. Mixed with benefit the undertaking can be carried out; mixed with harm the trouble can be resolved — by capping the line and measuring it (C6).
8. Value victory, not duration (my #8). A fixed N or a mark-only schedule is 巧之久 — skill that is prolonged — and the deadline does not move. F2's tracker is the "food from the enemy" of this design: it saved about a third of the cost it was paid to watch.
